{1}-{v}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent\n\nII. $\\{{v}_{1},2{v}_{2},3{v}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent\n\nIII. $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},0\\}$ is linearly independent" 13194139534796 17592186045417 true] [17592186373540 1071 "Consider a vector space $V$ and vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\in V.$ Given that ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${{v}_{1}-{v}_{2},{v}_{2}-{v}_{3},{v}_{1}-{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent\n- ${{v}_{1},2{v}_{2},3{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent\n- ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},0}$ is linearly independent" 13194139534796 17592186045417 false] [17592186373540 1074 "Let's examine each of the given options in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n1\\cdot ({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})+1\\cdot ({v}_{2}-{v}_{3})+(-1)\\cdot ({v}_{1}-{v}_{3})=0.\n$$\n\nAs a result, ${x}_{1}({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})+{x}_{2}({v}_{2}-{v}_{3})+{x}_{3}({v}_{1}-{v}_{3})=0$ has a non-zero solution. This means that $\\{{v}_{1}-{v}_{2},{v}_{2}-{v}_{3},{v}_{1}-{v}_{3}\\}$ is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}(2{v}_{2})+{x}_{3}(3{v}_{3}) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, in turn, gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+3{x}_{3}{v}_{3} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is a linearly independent set, the final equation can be true only if\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe unique solution of the system is ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0,$ which means that \n\n$$\n\\{{v}_{1},2{v}_{2},3{v}_{3}\\}\n$$\n\n is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, notice that \n\n$$\n0\\cdot {v}_{1}+0\\cdot {v}_{2}+1\\cdot 0=0.\n$$\n\nAs a result, ${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}0=0$ has a non-zero solution. This means that $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},0\\}$ is linearly dependent. In general, any set containing the zero vector is linearly dependent.